Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — scene draft
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-09-28
Status: SC008 proposed for individual author scene review; combined pass provisionally accepted

INT./EXT. ELIAS'S CAR/MOTORWAY REFUGE - DAY

Elias mounts his phone where Zoe can see the destination. The route estimate climbs past six hours. From the rear seat she tests the handle and belt, then checks every airport turn against the blue line. Elias drives without hiding the screen.

On the motorway a dark saloon stays behind them through two junctions, then disappears behind a lorry. Zoe notices it. Elias has seen too little to identify its occupants. A withheld number rings; he lets it go. Zoe watches him keep the phone in its mount.

Heat fills the old car. Zoe swallows, pales and finally leans forward.

ZOE
I feel sick.

Elias indicates for the next emergency refuge. Zoe reaches for the belt before they stop.

ELIAS
Keep it on until we're stopped. Then you can open the door.

The hatchback stops. Zoe gets out with her backpack and retches by the barrier. Elias puts on the hazards, places a sealed bottle of water where she can see it, and stays by the car. She checks the cap before drinking.

ELIAS
Next services. Food, cameras, staff, people.

ZOE
People don't help.

ELIAS
Sometimes they complicate things.

Back on the road, the withheld number calls again. Elias rejects it. Zoe hears him refuse the call while moving. A tiny laugh escapes her at his dry reply about making Mac wait. He does not turn to catch it.

EXT./INT. SERVICE STATION/RESTAURANT - AFTERNOON

The dark saloon enters half a minute after them and disappears behind the coaches. Elias circles the car park and stops beneath cameras, in sight of the restaurant. He shows Zoe the public exits and kitchen access on the posted fire diagram. She insists on seeing the phone if he makes a call. He agrees to tell her first.

Inside THE YE OLD BARN, a WAITRESS offers a booth or window table. Zoe wants sight of outside; Elias points out the booth's mirrored view of the entrance and toilet corridor. Zoe chooses the booth and her side of it. Her backpack stays beside her, a strap around her wrist.

Zoe is vegetarian. The waitress recommends a baked potato with beans, no butter, and sealed water. Elias orders pie and tea. When a butter packet arrives on Zoe's plate, the waitress replaces the whole plate on a clean tray.

ZOE
Thank you.

She eats while watching the mirror. For a moment Elias's unpressed jokes bring a smile. The room keeps moving around them: families, drivers, staff. Zoe speaks again, quieter.

ZOE
I thought the route was compromised before the airport. There was a decoy.

ELIAS
Who knew the real one?

Her face changes. Elias lets the question go. She looks at the woman serving them, then back at him.

ZOE
Do you believe me?

ELIAS
I believe you think it was exposed. I believe someone followed us from the airport. And I believe you had a reason to run before you knew whether I was dangerous.

ZOE
Trust needs proof?

ELIAS
Yes. It's the only kind worth having.

Zoe tests that answer in silence. A figure crosses the far edge of the car park. Elias sees it in the window reflection, but cannot identify anyone yet.
